export default {"id":"dsp-level-0","number":0,"title":"DSP in this studio","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"1. Lecture 2 definition: digital as a method of representing a quantity; signal as a quantity that carries information; processing as numerical operations on that representation 2. Block diagram: x_c(t) → A-to-D → x[n] → computer → y[n] → D","modules":[{"id":"dsp-m-1-1","code":"1","title":"Audio DSP Fundamentals — Course Syllabus (draft v1 — full map)","blurb":"This document is the course map. It is not the lessons.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-guide-overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"How to read this syllabus overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Audio DSP Fundamentals — Course Syllabus (draft v1 — full map)","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Track id:</strong> <code>dsp</code>   <strong>App title (proposed):</strong> Audio DSP Fundamentals   <strong>Studio this course is written for:</strong> Ableton Live 12 <strong>Standard</strong> + <strong>Operator</strong> + <strong>Max for Live</strong> add-on · <strong>Windows</strong> and <strong>macOS</strong> only  "},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Status:</strong> Syllabus draft <strong>v1</strong>. Full textbook-density course map. Not lesson content. Not wired into the app.   <strong>Quality bar:</strong> Textbook density, cited, no invented constants, no Suite gear treated as installed, no operating system named other than Windows and macOS.   <strong>Grounding rule:</strong> Claims come from (1) <code>B:\\Documents\\TRAINING DOCS</code> + <code>B:\\Documents\\User Manuals</code>, (2) Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual (especially ch. 37 Computer Audio Resources, ch. 38 Audio Fact Sheet, ch. 28 Live Audio Effect Reference), (3) official Ableton Knowledge Base pages named below, (4) Davide Rocchesso, <em>Introduction to Sound Processing</em> (local PDF), (5) Digital Speech Processing Lecture 2 — <em>Review of DSP Fundamentals</em> (local), (6) named papers in the local Audio Analysis_Editing shelf, (7) Julius O. Smith III, <em>Mathematics of the Discrete Fourier Transform</em> (CCRMA, official web), (8) EBU R 128-2023 and ITU-R BS.1770 for loudness, (9) Apple Core Audio documentation for the macOS driver layer. If retrieval cannot support a needed fact, the lesson emits a <code>{type:'gap'}</code> block."},{"type":"prose","html":"This document is the course map. It is <strong>not</strong> the lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Tone:</strong> Neutral textbook prose. Declarative exposition and imperative procedure only. No coaching voice, metaphors, or motivational asides."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Approximate size of this map:</strong> 15 levels (0–14) · ~48 modules · Gates A–K · Capstone."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Platform lock:</strong> Instruction, paths, shortcuts, and compatibility statements address <strong>Windows</strong> and <strong>macOS</strong> only. Other operating systems are out of scope and are not named in teaching material."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-0-2","code":"0","title":"How to read this syllabus","blurb":"1. Work in order through Levels 0 → 14, then Capstone. 2. Each module lists learning outcomes, topic coverage, mustCover / mustNotCover, primary sources, practice, and a self-assessment checkpoint.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-guide-1","title":"How to read this syllabus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0. How to read this syllabus","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Work <strong>in order</strong> through Levels 0 → 14, then Capstone.","Each module lists <strong>learning outcomes</strong>, <strong>topic coverage</strong>, <strong>mustCover / mustNotCover</strong>, <strong>primary sources</strong>, <strong>practice</strong>, and a <strong>self-assessment checkpoint</strong>.","Checkpoints are <strong>self-assessment</strong>. The app cannot observe Live’s audio engine, Spectrum, or an interface control panel. Do not invent <code>.als</code> graders or automatic FFT scorers.","Depth registers (Plain / Standard / Technical) live <strong>inside</strong> a step and must not change step count.","<strong>Sister courses</strong> own overlapping craft — this course <strong>points</strong>, it does not duplicate:","<strong>Sound Design Foundations</strong> — synthesis and timbre craft (oscillators, FM, wavetables). This course explains the <em>signal processing</em> underneath filters, delays, and analyzers; it does not teach Operator patch design.","<strong>Building Max for Live Devices</strong> — implementing DSP as <code>.amxd</code> devices. This course does <strong>not</strong> require writing Max patches.","<strong>Mastering Ableton Live 12</strong> — host, browser, and the full device encyclopedia as a Live <em>user</em>.","<strong>Foundations of Music Production</strong> — song form, Session vs Arrangement, finishing.","<strong>Spatial Audio &amp; Dolby Atmos</strong> — multichannel spatial formats.","<strong>AI Tools for Music Production</strong> — operating stem/generation tools, not DSP theory.","Numbered topic lists under modules are <strong>coverage outlines</strong> for densified lesson pages, not one micro-page per bullet by default."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"0.1 What “Audio DSP Fundamentals” means here","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Digital signal processing (DSP) is the representation of a physical quantity as a sequence of numbers, and the numerical operations performed on that sequence. In this studio the physical quantity is sound pressure converted to a voltage by a microphone or instrument, then converted to a discrete-time, discrete-amplitude sequence by an analog-to-digital converter (ADC). After processing, a digital-to-analog converter (DAC) reconstructs a continuous voltage for speakers or headphones."},{"type":"prose","html":"Lecture 2 of <em>Digital Speech Processing</em> states the chain as:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">continuous input  →  A-to-D  →  computer  →  D-to-A  →  continuous output\n     x_c(t)              x[n]        y[n]              y_c(t)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"A-to-D performs bandwidth control, sampling, and quantization. The computer performs finite-precision arithmetic (add, multiply, convolution, difference equations, DFT). D-to-A performs re-quantization and reconstruction filtering."},{"type":"prose","html":"The curriculum trains one transferable model:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">CONTINUOUS SOUND\n    → SAMPLE (time discretization; Nyquist)\n    → QUANTIZE (amplitude discretization; word length; dither)\n    → PROCESS (LTI: convolution / filters / delay)\n           or NONLINEAR (waveshape, clip, saturate)\n           or TIME-VARYING (dynamics, modulated delay, STFT)\n    → MEASURE (peak, RMS, LUFS, Spectrum)\n    → RENDER (bit depth, sample-rate conversion, dither once)\n    → RECONSTRUCT (DAC)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Every later module hangs on that chain."}]},{"type":"step","title":"0.2 Suggested pace (flexible — mastery over calendar)","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Level","Focus","Approx. duration","Gate"],"rows":[["0","What DSP is; this studio’s audio path","3–5 days","—"],["1","Continuous sound, frequency, amplitude, decibels","3–7 days","—"],["2","Sampling, Nyquist, aliasing, reconstruction","1–2 weeks","A"],["3","Quantization, bit depth, dither, PCM vs float","1–2 weeks","B"],["4","Discrete-time signals, LTI systems, convolution","1–2 weeks","C"],["5","DFT / FFT, windows, Spectrum","1–2 weeks","D"],["6","Digital filters: FIR, IIR, types, phase","2–3 weeks","E"],["7","Delay lines and delay-based effects","1–2 weeks","F"],["8","Dynamics processors as time-varying gain","1–2 weeks","G"],["9","Nonlinear processing and harmonic aliasing","1–2 weeks","—"],["10","STFT, phase vocoder, Live warp modes","2–3 weeks","H"],["11","Reverberation algorithms","1–2 weeks","I"],["12","Multirate processing and resampling","1 week","—"],["13","Real-time constraints: buffer, latency, zipper, CPU","1–2 weeks","J"],["14","Metering: peak, RMS, LUFS, true peak","1–2 weeks","K"],["Capstone","Documented processing chain with measurements","1–2 weeks","Capstone"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Mastery of checkpoints matters more than calendar speed."}]},{"type":"step","title":"0.3 How lessons should feel when authored later","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["One new variable at a time. Change sample rate <strong>or</strong> bit depth <strong>or</strong> filter cutoff, not all three.","Every abstract claim is verified in Live with <strong>Operator</strong> (sine / noise source) and <strong>Spectrum</strong> (or a second meter where the module names one).","A/B the processed signal against the dry signal using Utility gain or a return, not by guessing.","Numbers that appear in a lesson (Hz, dB, samples, LUFS) must appear in that lesson’s cited source, or be a direct arithmetic consequence of a sourced formula (for example <code>Nyquist = Fs / 2</code>, <code>delay_seconds = delay_samples / Fs</code>).","Cross-platform checks mean <strong>Windows and macOS</strong> only."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-1-3","code":"1","title":"Locked studio facts (do not drift)","blurb":"1. Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual — ch. 38 Audio Fact Sheet, ch. 37 Computer Audio Resources, ch. 28 Live Audio Effect Reference, plus Settings / Audio facts in Ableton KB 2. Miranda studio lock: Standard + Operator + Max for Live; Window","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-guide-2","title":"Locked studio facts (do not drift)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1 Edition and ownership","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Fact","This course"],"rows":[["DAW","Live 12 <strong>Standard</strong> + <strong>Operator</strong> + <strong>Max for Live</strong> add-on (or Suite, which includes Max for Live)"],["Role of Operator","Test-tone and noise <strong>source</strong> only (sine, other waveforms, noise). Not a synthesis-craft course"],["Role of Max for Live","Installed; <strong>not</strong> required as an authoring environment. Do not assign “build and save a new Max device”"],["Analysis devices (Standard)","<strong>Spectrum</strong>, <strong>Tuner</strong>, <strong>Utility</strong>"],["Filter / EQ devices (Standard)","<strong>EQ Eight</strong>, <strong>Channel EQ</strong>, <strong>Auto Filter</strong>"],["Dynamics (Standard)","<strong>Compressor</strong>, <strong>Glue Compressor</strong>, <strong>Gate</strong>, <strong>Limiter</strong>"],["Time / modulation (Standard)","<strong>Delay</strong>, <strong>Reverb</strong>, <strong>Chorus-Ensemble</strong>, <strong>Phaser-Flanger</strong>, <strong>Shifter</strong>"],["Nonlinear (Standard)","<strong>Saturator</strong>, <strong>Overdrive</strong>, <strong>Dynamic Tube</strong>"],["Do <strong>not</strong> teach as installed","<strong>Hybrid Reverb</strong>, <strong>Echo</strong>, <strong>Amp</strong>, <strong>Cabinet</strong>, <strong>Roar</strong>, <strong>Sampler</strong>, Live <strong>Wavetable</strong>, <strong>Granulator III</strong>, <strong>Stem Separation</strong>, Spectral Time / Spectral Resonator as required student tools"],["Platforms","<strong>Windows</strong> and <strong>macOS</strong> only"],["Windows driver","<strong>ASIO</strong> (manufacturer driver). Ableton: “the best performing audio driver type is ASIO.” <strong>MME/DirectX are not recommended.</strong> ASIO4ALL / FlexASIO are Ableton-documented fallbacks when a device has no proprietary ASIO driver — optional, not required"],["macOS driver","<strong>CoreAudio</strong> (Apple’s audio HAL). Some interfaces need no extra software (Ableton <em>Setting up an Audio Interface</em>)"],["Default sample rate in Live","<strong>44100 Hz</strong> (Ableton <em>Setting up an Audio Interface</em>)"],["Buffer size","Powers of two starting at <strong>32</strong> samples. Ableton recommends <strong>256 or 512</strong> as a practical compromise; the Windows optimization article also lists <strong>128, 256, 512, 1024</strong>"],["Internal Live processing","<strong>32-bit</strong>. Summing at each mix point is <strong>64-bit</strong> (Audio Fact Sheet §38.2.4)"],["Export / freeze word length","Freeze files are <strong>32-bit</strong>. Ableton recommends rendering at <strong>32-bit</strong> when finishing in Live"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Resolution order for every later lesson:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual — ch. <strong>38 Audio Fact Sheet</strong>, ch. <strong>37 Computer Audio Resources</strong>, ch. <strong>28 Live Audio Effect Reference</strong>, plus Settings / Audio facts in Ableton KB  ","Miranda studio lock: Standard + Operator + Max for Live; Windows and macOS only  ","Local DSP shelf (<code>TRAINING DOCS\\…\\Audio Analysis_Editing\\</code> and the identical tree under <code>TRAINING DOCS\\AI\\</code>) — Rocchesso, Lecture 2, convolution tutorial, FIR linear-phase paper, delay-effects paper, Dolson phase vocoder, Dattorro AES reverb, zipper-noise note  ","Julius O. Smith III, CCRMA <em>Mathematics of the DFT</em> (sampling theorem, sinc reconstruction) — official web, used to fill gaps the local PDFs leave in the sampling proof  ","EBU R 128-2023 + ITU-R BS.1770 for loudness units and true peak — official web  ","Apple Core Audio documentation (macOS HAL / linear PCM) and Ableton Windows ASIO articles  ","Training / NotebookLM exports — supporting only; never override manuals or the standards above  "],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"If a knowledgebase says “Operator is Suite-only,” that is Ableton’s shop matrix, not this studio. Teach Operator as the tone source."}]},{"type":"step","title":"1.2 Product definitions (factual)","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Term","Definition (source-aligned)"],"rows":[["<strong>Signal</strong>","A detectable physical quantity that carries information (Lecture 2). Here: a sound-pressure / voltage / sample sequence"],["<strong>Digital</strong>","A representation of a quantity by a finite set of symbols — in this course, binary words (Lecture 2; Rocchesso §1.6)"],["<strong>Sampling</strong>","Replacing a continuous-time signal <code>x_c(t)</code> by the sequence <code>x[n] = x_c(nT)</code>, where <code>T</code> is the sampling period and <code>Fs = 1/T</code> is the sampling frequency (Lecture 2)"],["<strong>Nyquist frequency</strong>","<code>Fs / 2</code>. A bandlimited signal can be reconstructed exactly if <code>Fs ≥ 2 f_max</code> (Lecture 2; Smith, Sampling Theorem; Rocchesso §1.2)"],["<strong>Aliasing</strong>","Spectral replicas created by sampling fold into the baseband when the signal is not bandlimited below <code>Fs/2</code> (Rocchesso §1.3; Smith aliasing theorem)"],["<strong>Quantization</strong>","Mapping a continuous amplitude onto one of <code>2^b</code> reconstruction levels for a <code>b</code>-bit word (Rocchesso §1.6; Lecture 2)"],["<strong>LTI system</strong>","Linear and time-invariant. Completely described by its impulse response. Output is the convolution of input with that impulse response (Rocchesso §1.4)"],["<strong>Convolution</strong>","<code>y[n] = (h * x)[n] = Σ x[m] h[n−m]</code>. Time-domain convolution ↔ frequency-domain multiplication (Rocchesso §1.4.1; Kim convolution tutorial)"],["<strong>DFT</strong>","Discrete Fourier Transform of an <code>N</code>-sample window: <code>Y(k) = Σ_{n=0}^{N−1} y(n) e^{−j 2π kn / N}</code>, bins at <code>f_k = k Fs / N</code> (Rocchesso §1.3)"],["<strong>FFT</strong>","Fast algorithm for the DFT, complexity on the order of <code>N log N</code> rather than <code>N²</code> (Rocchesso §1.3; Sundararajan DFT notes)"],["<strong>FIR</strong>","Finite impulse response filter — a tapped delay line. Real-valued linear-phase FIR filters require symmetric coefficients <code>h(n) = h(N−1−n)</code> (Lyons; Rocchesso ch. 2)"],["<strong>IIR</strong>","Infinite impulse response filter — recursive. Poles must lie inside the unit circle for BIBO stability of a causal discrete-time LTI system (Rocchesso §1.4.3, §2.2)"],["<strong>Phase vocoder</strong>","Analysis–synthesis model of a signal as a sum of sinusoids with time-varying amplitude and frequency. Filter-bank view and STFT/FFT view are mathematically equivalent (Dolson 1986; Flanagan &amp; Golden 1966)"],["<strong>LUFS</strong>","Loudness Units referenced to Full Scale. EBU name for the unit ITU-R BS.1770 calls LKFS. Equivalent (EBU R 128-2023, note 1)"],["<strong>True Peak (dBTP)</strong>","Maximum of the reconstructed continuous-time waveform, not the sample-peak. EBU R 128: shall not exceed <strong>−1 dBTP</strong> in production (linear audio)"],["<strong>Neutral operation (Live)</strong>","A Live procedure that causes no change in audio quality under stated conditions (Audio Fact Sheet §38.2). Gain change is <em>not</em> neutral"],["<strong>ASIO</strong>","Audio Stream Input/Output. Ableton’s recommended driver type on <strong>Windows</strong>"],["<strong>Core Audio</strong>","Apple’s audio HAL on <strong>macOS</strong>. Live’s Driver Type on Mac is <strong>CoreAudio</strong>. Core Audio data structures describe linear PCM at any sample rate and bit depth (Apple)"]],"caption":null}]},{"type":"step","title":"1.3 Mental model every lesson reinforces","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">INTERFACE  (ADC / DAC, manufacturer control panel)\n    ↕  ASIO (Windows)  or  CoreAudio (macOS)\nLIVE AUDIO ENGINE\n    sample rate Fs (default 44100 Hz)\n    buffer N samples (powers of two)\n    32-bit processing · 64-bit mix-point summing\n        └── track chain:  MIDI effects → Instrument → Audio effects\n                ├── linear DSP (EQ, filters, delay, reverb, Utility)\n                ├── time-varying DSP (compressor, chorus, warp)\n                └── meters (Spectrum, level, loudness)\nEXPORT / FREEZE\n    same Fs + 32-bit  →  can be neutral\n    lower bit depth   →  dither once\n    different Fs      →  SoX resampler (non-neutral)</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"1.4 Keyboard and path conventions","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Action","Windows","macOS"],"rows":[["Live Settings","Ctrl + ,","Cmd + ,"],["Audio tab","Settings → Audio","Settings → Audio"],["Driver type","<strong>ASIO</strong>","<strong>CoreAudio</strong>"],["Interface control panel","Settings → Audio → <strong>Hardware Setup</strong> (often required to change buffer / Fs)","Interface app if the maker provides one; many devices need none"],["Spectrum / devices","Browser → Audio Effects","Browser → Audio Effects"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"User Library and interface-installer paths differ by OS; lessons state both when file locations matter. Do not invent absolute system paths."}]},{"type":"step","title":"1.5 Honesty rules","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Do not mention any operating system other than <strong>Windows</strong> and <strong>macOS</strong>.","Do not treat Suite-only devices as student tools.","Do not require MATLAB, Octave, or writing C/Max/Gen code. Rocchesso’s MATLAB listings are <em>source illustrations</em>, not course homework.","Do not invent a guaranteed round-trip latency in milliseconds. Teach <code>t_buffer = N / Fs</code> as the <strong>I/O buffer contribution only</strong>. Total latency also includes the interface safety offset and any plugin delay compensation (Ableton latency documentation).","Do not claim a filter topology, warp algorithm, or reverb structure for a Live device unless the Live manual states it. Live’s Reverb is taught as Live’s Reverb; Dattorro / Schroeder are taught as <em>algorithm families</em>, not as “this is what Live Reverb is.”","Do not claim Complex / Complex Pro warp is transparent. Audio Fact Sheet §38.3.1: those modes are <strong>never neutral</strong>, even at the original tempo.","Do not apply dither more than once, and do not dither 32-bit renders (Audio Fact Sheet §38.3.4).","Do not present EBU −23 LUFS as a “music mastering law.” It is the <strong>broadcast Programme Loudness</strong> target. Streaming platforms publish their own targets; cite the platform document if a later lesson names one, otherwise emit <code>{type:'gap'}</code>.","A number appears in a lesson only if a cited source states it or it is direct arithmetic from a sourced formula."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":9}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-2-4","code":"2","title":"Pedagogical spine (why this order)","blurb":"1. Identity of DSP and this studio’s converters before any equation 2. Continuous sound and decibels so later plots have units","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-guide-3","title":"Pedagogical spine (why this order)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2. Pedagogical spine (why this order)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Identity of DSP</strong> and this studio’s converters before any equation  ","<strong>Continuous sound and decibels</strong> so later plots have units  ","<strong>Sampling</strong> before quantization (time grid before amplitude grid)  ","<strong>Quantization and dither</strong> before claiming “32-bit is cleaner”  ","<strong>LTI / convolution</strong> before naming any filter  ","<strong>DFT / Spectrum</strong> before designing filters by ear-plus-plot  ","<strong>FIR vs IIR</strong> before delay-based effects (delay is the FIR building block)  ","<strong>Modulated delay</strong> before dynamics (both are time-varying; delay is easier to hear)  ","<strong>Dynamics</strong> as gain computed from a detector, not as “make it punchy”  ","<strong>Nonlinear</strong> after linear, so harmonic aliasing has a vocabulary  ","<strong>STFT / phase vocoder / warp</strong> after the DFT  ","<strong>Reverb</strong> after delay, allpass, and feedback are known  ","<strong>Multirate / SRC</strong> after the sampling theorem  ","<strong>Real-time constraints</strong> after the student can name what the CPU is computing  ","<strong>Loudness standards</strong> last, so meters are read against a named recommendation  "],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Rule:</strong> A concept that cannot be demonstrated with Operator + a Standard audio effect + Spectrum (or a named meter) is not finished."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-3-5","code":"3","title":"Course map at a glance","blurb":"Course map at a glance","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-guide-4","title":"Course map at a glance","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3. Course map at a glance","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Level","Title","Modules (approx.)","Gate"],"rows":[["0","DSP in this studio","3","—"],["1","Continuous sound, frequency, decibels","3","—"],["2","Sampling, Nyquist, aliasing","3","<strong>A</strong>"],["3","Quantization, dither, word length","3","<strong>B</strong>"],["4","Discrete-time systems and convolution","3","<strong>C</strong>"],["5","DFT, FFT, windows, Spectrum","3","<strong>D</strong>"],["6","Digital filters","4","<strong>E</strong>"],["7","Delay lines and time-based effects","3","<strong>F</strong>"],["8","Dynamics as DSP","3","<strong>G</strong>"],["9","Nonlinear processing","3","—"],["10","STFT, phase vocoder, warp","3","<strong>H</strong>"],["11","Reverberation","3","<strong>I</strong>"],["12","Multirate and resampling","3","—"],["13","Real-time constraints","3","<strong>J</strong>"],["14","Metering and loudness","3","<strong>K</strong>"],["Capstone","Measured processing chain","1","Capstone"]],"caption":null}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-4-6","code":"4","title":"Assessment model","blurb":"Assessment model","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-guide-5","title":"Assessment model","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4. Assessment model","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Layer","Design"],"rows":[["Per idea","Check after every concept (MCQ, plot-read, order, calculation)"],["Per lesson","End-of-lesson self-check + Live homework"],["Per module","Checkpoint checklist (self-assessment)"],["Gates A–K","Progressive competence (see §5)"],["Capstone","Written chain diagram + exported files + meter readings + source citations"],["Depth","Plain / Standard / Technical inside steps"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Forbidden:</strong> fake auto-grading of Live tasks the app cannot observe."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-5-7","code":"5","title":"Gates","blurb":"Gates","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-guide-6","title":"Gates","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5. Gates","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Gate","After","Pass means (self-assessment)"],"rows":[["<strong>A</strong>","L2","State <code>Fs ≥ 2 f_max</code>; compute Nyquist at 44.1 kHz and 48 kHz; demonstrate aliasing of a sine swept past Nyquist (or folded by lowering Fs) and name the anti-alias filter’s job"],["<strong>B</strong>","L3","State <code>2^b</code> levels; explain why Live processes at 32-bit; state when dither is required and that it is applied <strong>once</strong>; contrast sample-peak with quantization error"],["<strong>C</strong>","L4","Write the discrete convolution sum; identify an impulse response by sending a click / impulse through a linear effect; state time-domain convolution ↔ frequency-domain multiply"],["<strong>D</strong>","L5","Compute bin width <code>Fs / N</code>; read a Spectrum plot of a sine and of a square-ish Operator wave; name main-lobe vs side-lobe as a windowing fact"],["<strong>E</strong>","L6","Distinguish FIR vs IIR by impulse-response length and by phase; name LP / HP / BP / notch; state the real-FIR linear-phase symmetry condition"],["<strong>F</strong>","L7","Draw delay + mix (comb); distinguish flanger (modulated short delay + feedback, harmonic comb) from chorus (several fractional delays) from phaser (allpass notches, inharmonic) using the delay-effects paper"],["<strong>G</strong>","L8","Name detector → gain computer → applied gain; state that bypassed Live effects with lookahead still introduce that delay (Fact Sheet §38.2.8)"],["<strong>H</strong>","L10","State that Complex / Complex Pro are <strong>never neutral</strong>; describe time-scale vs pitch-shift in the phase-vocoder model (Dolson)"],["<strong>I</strong>","L11","Name predelay, decay, damping, diffusion as reverberator controls (Dattorro); contrast algorithmic recirculation with convolution against an impulse response"],["<strong>J</strong>","L13","Set ASIO (Windows) or CoreAudio (macOS); change buffer among Ableton’s recommended sizes; explain CPU meter as buffer-compute-time / buffer-play-time (Manual §37.1.1)"],["<strong>K</strong>","L14","Distinguish sample peak, true peak, RMS, and LUFS; state EBU R 128 Programme Loudness <strong>−23.0 LUFS</strong> and true-peak ceiling <strong>−1 dBTP</strong> as <em>broadcast</em> figures, not as a universal music-master law"],["<strong>Capstone</strong>","after L14","See §7"]],"caption":null}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-1-1-2","code":"1","title":"overview dsp part 1","blurb":"overview dsp part 1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-1-overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"LEVEL 0 — DSP in this studio overview","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective:</strong> Establish what DSP is, how converters sit around Live, and which devices this course is allowed to use."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-0-1-2","code":"0.1","title":"— What DSP is","blurb":"1. Lecture 2 definition: digital as a method of representing a quantity; signal as a quantity that carries information; processing as numerical operations on that representation 2. Block diagram: x_c(t) → A-to-D → x[n] → computer → y[n] → D","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-1-1","title":"What DSP is","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 0.1 — What DSP is","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Define digital, signal, and processing; draw the ADC → computer → DAC chain."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Lecture 2 definition: digital as a method of representing a quantity; signal as a quantity that carries information; processing as numerical operations on that representation","Block diagram: <code>x_c(t) → A-to-D → x[n] → computer → y[n] → D-to-A → y_c(t)</code>","A-to-D jobs: bandwidth control (anti-alias), sampling, quantization","Computer jobs: add / multiply (gain), convolution / linear filtering, nonlinear filtering, difference equations, DFT","D-to-A jobs: re-quantization and reconstruction filtering","Why digital: permanence of the representation; flexibility of numerical algorithms (Lecture 2). Do not claim “zero distortion” as a universal property — Lecture 2 says zero-distortion <em>may</em> be achievable; Live’s Fact Sheet is explicit that many everyday operations are non-neutral"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> The three-block chain; sampling vs quantization as two different discretizations   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> Writing code; Suite devices; any OS other than Windows/macOS   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Digital Speech Processing Lecture 2, slides “What is DSP?”, “Digital Processing of Analog Signals,” “Advantages of Digital Representations”   <strong>Practice:</strong> Draw the chain and label <code>x_c(t)</code>, <code>x[n]</code>, <code>y[n]</code>, <code>y_c(t)</code>   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner names sampling and quantization as two separate steps inside A-to-D"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-0-2-3","code":"0.2","title":"— This studio’s converters and drivers","blurb":"1. Windows: Driver Type = ASIO. Install the interface manufacturer’s ASIO driver. MME/DirectX not recommended (Ableton Optimizing Windows for Audio) 2. macOS: Driver Type = CoreAudio. Some interfaces need no extra software (Ableton Setting ","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-1-2","title":"This studio’s converters and drivers","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 0.2 — This studio’s converters and drivers","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Open Live’s Audio settings on the student’s OS; select the correct driver type; state the default sample rate."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Windows:</strong> Driver Type = <strong>ASIO</strong>. Install the interface manufacturer’s ASIO driver. MME/DirectX not recommended (Ableton <em>Optimizing Windows for Audio</em>)","<strong>macOS:</strong> Driver Type = <strong>CoreAudio</strong>. Some interfaces need no extra software (Ableton <em>Setting up an Audio Interface</em>). Core Audio is Apple’s hardware-abstraction layer for linear PCM (Apple Core Audio documentation)","Default In/Out sample rate in Live: <strong>44100 Hz</strong>. Higher rates are available from the chooser if the hardware supports them","Buffer size: powers of two from 32 samples. Lower buffer → less I/O latency contribution and higher CPU. Ableton recommends <strong>256 or 512</strong> as a compromise","On Windows, buffer and sometimes sample rate may be greyed out in Live — change them in the interface control panel via <strong>Hardware Setup</strong> (Ableton <em>Changing the buffer size/sample rate of an ASIO audio interface</em>)","Activate only the inputs and outputs you need (Manual §37.1.2). Live does not auto-disable unused channels because many drivers hiccup on configuration changes","Optional Windows fallback: ASIO4ALL or FlexASIO, only as Ableton documents them, only if the hardware has no proprietary ASIO driver"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> ASIO on Windows; CoreAudio on macOS; 44100 Hz default; 256/512 recommendation; Hardware Setup on Windows   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> Naming any third OS; claiming a universal latency in ms; requiring ASIO4ALL   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Ableton <em>Setting up an Audio Interface</em>; <em>Optimizing Windows for Audio</em>; <em>Changing the buffer size/sample rate… (Windows)</em>; Apple Core Audio overview; Live Manual §37.1.2   <strong>Practice:</strong> Screenshot (for the student’s notes) of Settings → Audio showing driver type, device, 44100 Hz, and a recommended buffer   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner states the driver type for their OS and the default sample rate"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-0-3-4","code":"0.3","title":"— Live’s audio engine facts and device lock","blurb":"1. Live processes at 32-bit. Mix points (clip inputs, returns, Main, Racks) use 64-bit summing (Fact Sheet §38.2.4) 2. Neutral vs non-neutral operations — preview of Level 3 and Level 12. Even a gain change is non-neutral","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-1-3","title":"Live’s audio engine facts and device lock","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 0.3 — Live’s audio engine facts and device lock","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> State Live’s internal word length and mix-point summing; list the Standard devices this course uses."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Live processes at <strong>32-bit</strong>. Mix points (clip inputs, returns, Main, Racks) use <strong>64-bit</strong> summing (Fact Sheet §38.2.4)","Neutral vs non-neutral operations — preview of Level 3 and Level 12. Even a gain change is non-neutral","Bypassed effects are removed from the signal flow, except devices whose parameters inherently require delay (e.g. Compressor lookahead), which keep that delay for compensation (Fact Sheet §38.2.8)","Device lock for labs: Operator (source), Spectrum, Utility, EQ Eight, Auto Filter, Compressor, Delay, Reverb, Chorus-Ensemble, Phaser-Flanger, Shifter, Saturator","Sister-course boundary: this course does not teach Operator as FM craft; it does not build Max devices"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> 32-bit process / 64-bit mix; Standard device list; bypass-plus-lookahead exception   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> Hybrid Reverb / Echo / Amp as student tools   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Live 12 Manual §38.2.4, §38.2.8; ch. 28 device list; studio lock   <strong>Practice:</strong> Load Operator → Spectrum on a MIDI track; confirm sound and a visible peak   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner names the internal word length and three Standard devices this course will use"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Gate:</strong> none (orientation)"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
